        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>FOX</strong> (Hebrews shu’al, a name derived from its digging or burrowing under ground), the Vulpes thaleb, or Syrian fox, the only species of this animal indigenous to Palestine. It burrows, is silent and solitary in its habits, is destructive to vineyards, being a plunderer of ripe grapes (Cant. 2:15). The Vulpes Niloticus, or Egyptian dog-fox, and the Vulpes vulgaris, or common fox, are also found in Palestine. The proverbial cunning of the fox is alluded to in Ezekiel 13:4, and in… <em>[Reviewer: condense Easton's entry as needed.]</em>",
